<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
    <!--
      Soil-sampling drill, from the 2026-08-19 CAD re-export (new_mesh/GLB/
      drill.glb), which names four groups: drill_body, drill_motor, drill_bit
      and drill_container. It supersedes the single 8-material blob the old
      drill.glb/drill.stl carried (now in meshes/unused/), which had no moving
      parts at all.

      FRAME. The export is in the CAD assembly frame, not base_link - it sits
      around (0.87, 1.32, 1.65) m with the mast running along CAD +Y. Every
      mesh below was baked into base_link coordinates on export with

          x_base = z_cad - 2.0696
          y_base = x_cad - 0.8975
          z_base = y_cad - 0.9262

      i.e. a proper rotation (rpy 1.5708 0 1.5708) plus that offset. The
      mapping was fixed by matching the four mast extrusions, the bottom plate
      (z = -0.022), the top plate (z = 0.6825) and the deck-level cross member
      (z = 0.2122..0.232) against the old drill mesh, which was already correct
      in base_link. The sign is not a free choice: the alternative that also
      matches the (symmetric) endpoints is a reflection, det -1, which no rigid
      CAD export can be.

      Because the meshes are already in base_link coordinates, every joint
      origin here is 0 0 0 and each link frame is coincident with base_link at
      q = 0. That is legitimate for prismatic joints - the axis passes through
      the origin - and it is what the previous drill.xacro did for the fixed
      case. Inertial origins are therefore also in base_link coordinates.

      drill_bit.glb is the ONE exception, and it has to be: it spins, and a
      revolute axis passes through its link's origin, so a bit left in base_link
      coordinates would orbit the rover's centreline half a metre away instead
      of turning on itself. That mesh was re-datumed onto its own axis, so its
      joint carries the axis position and its visual/inertial origins are local.
    -->

    <!-- ================================================================
         Mast, frame and everything bolted to it. Carries no inertial: its
         mass is still part of base_link's aggregate, as it always has been.
         ================================================================ -->
    <link name="drill_link">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_body.glb"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_body.stl"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
    </link>

    <joint name="drill_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="base_link"/>
        <child link="drill_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ================================================================
         FEED CARRIAGE (CAD "drill_motor"): the drill head, its motor, the
         carriage plate and the ball nut. Rides four 10 mm guide rods at
         y = -0.0534 / +0.0551, x = -0.5945 / -0.4545, driven by the lead
         screw on the bit axis offset (x = -0.5095, y = 0.0551).

         Mass properties are volume x density, not a weighed figure: 485 cm^3
         at 4000 kg/m^3 = 1.94 kg, the average of costing the 6 mm aluminium
         plate at 2700, the motor/gearbox body at 5000 and the steel nut at
         7850 (1.99 kg that way). Replace all three drill masses with scale
         readings when the hardware is assembled - see the base_link note in
         aries_base.xacro, which was reduced by exactly the 4.3032 kg added
         here so the rover still totals 90 kg.
         ================================================================ -->
    <link name="drill_motor_link">
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="-0.52425 -0.01675 0.42704" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="1.9407"/>
            <inertia ixx="6.74274e-03" ixy="-9.72398e-04" ixz="2.73673e-04"
                     iyy="4.28647e-03" iyz="5.85743e-04" izz="8.66488e-03"/>
        </inertial>
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_motor.glb"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_motor.stl"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
    </link>

    <!--
      Travel measured by sweeping the carriage against the frame mesh, 2 mm
      steps, and watching where surface contact jumps off its sliding-fit
      baseline:

        DOWN  -0.377  the head's 58 mm nose (bottom z = 0.3613) lands on the
                      base plate at z = -0.016. It does NOT continue into the
                      50 mm bore the bit passes through - the nose is wider
                      than the bore.
        UP    +0.187  the carriage top (z = 0.4894) meets the mast's top plate
                      at z = 0.6765.

      Both are quoted 2 mm inside those numbers so the stop is the joint limit
      and not a mesh interpenetration. The up-stroke is not arbitrary: lifting
      the bit tip from z = -0.1291 clear of the container rim at z = 0.0495
      needs 0.1787, which is what +0.185 provides with 6 mm to spare. That is
      the sample-collection sequence - drill down, retract, run the container
      underneath - and it is the check that the two limits below are right.

      effort/velocity are placeholders sized for a lead-screw feed, not
      measured from the drive.
    -->
    <joint name="drill_motor_joint" type="prismatic">
        <parent link="drill_link"/>
        <child link="drill_motor_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
        <limit effort="200.0" velocity="0.05" lower="-0.375" upper="0.185"/>
        <dynamics damping="5.0" friction="2.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ================================================================
         AUGER (CAD "drill_bit"): 50 mm flighting on a 30 mm core, from
         z = 0.4225 down to z = -0.1291 on the bit axis (x = -0.5095,
         y = 0.0001) - 38 mm above the ground plane at the home pose, and it
         passes through the 50 mm bore in the base plate on the way down.

         VISUAL ONLY, no <collision>, as specified. It hangs off the carriage,
         so it feeds with the motor, and it spins on its own axis - see the
         joint below.

         It still needs an <inertial>: 2.15 kg is a real fraction of the
         carriage's swung mass, and a link with no inertial is given a 1 kg
         unit-tensor default by the SDF converter, which would be both wrong
         and silent. 274 cm^3 of steel at 7850 kg/m^3. Both tensor entries that
         matter check out: (1/12)mL^2 = 0.0546 against 0.0538 here for the
         slender-rod terms, and izz = 1.024e-3 sits above a solid cylinder's
         (1/2)mr^2 = 6.73e-4, which is what flighting carrying its mass out at
         the rim should do.

         Coordinates here are LOCAL to the bit, not base_link like every other
         mesh in this file: drill_bit.glb was re-datumed onto the rotation axis
         so the mesh origin is the axis. The COM lands 32 um / 46 um off it, so
         the spinning mass is balanced and there is no wobble forcing.
         ================================================================ -->
    <link name="drill_bit_link">
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0.000032 0.000046 0.143675" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="2.1539"/>
            <inertia ixx="5.38379e-02" ixy="-5.78053e-07" ixz="-7.33246e-06"
                     iyy="5.38335e-02" iyz="8.91002e-06" izz="1.02388e-03"/>
        </inertial>
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_bit.glb"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>
    </link>

    <!--
      Spindle rotation: continuous, so it turns either way without end, which is
      what a drill that has to back out of a hole needs.

      The origin is the one place in this file that is not 0 0 0, and it is not
      optional. A revolute axis runs through the CHILD link's origin, so with
      0 0 0 the bit would swing about base_link's centreline half a metre away
      rather than turning on itself. x = -0.509508, y = 0.000085 is the axis
      measured off the mesh, not eyeballed: the outer flighting holds
      r_max = 25.000 mm about it at every height, and the 30 mm core agrees to
      within 1 um. It is the same axis the lead screw is offset from and the
      same one the base plate's 50 mm bore is centred on.

      A continuous joint takes no lower/upper; effort and velocity are still
      declared because ros2_control and gz both read them. 60 rad/s is ~570 rpm,
      an upper bound for an auger of this size, and is a placeholder until the
      drive is specified. Damping is deliberately low - this axis is meant to
      spin freely, unlike the two feed axes.
    -->
    <joint name="drill_bit_joint" type="continuous">
        <parent link="drill_motor_link"/>
        <child link="drill_bit_link"/>
        <origin xyz="-0.509508 0.000085 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
        <limit effort="20.0" velocity="60.0"/>
        <dynamics damping="0.05" friction="0.02"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SAMPLE CONTAINER (CAD "drill_container"): the open bin plus its four
         feet and two cross plates, riding the base plate on rails. 77 cm^3 of
         wall at 2700 kg/m^3 - the mesh is a shell, not a solid, so the
         envelope's 607 cm^3 is not the figure to density.
         ================================================================ -->
    <link name="drill_container_link">
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="-0.37906 0.00013 0.01433" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="0.2086"/>
            <inertia ixx="1.74359e-04" ixy="1.35749e-07" ixz="-1.30521e-09"
                     iyy="3.58964e-04" iyz="1.04583e-07" izz="4.06765e-04"/>
        </inertial>
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_container.glb"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/drill_container.stl"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
    </link>

    <!--
      The bin parks forward of the mast (bin centre x = -0.3791) and runs back
      along -X to sit under the bit: the bit axis is at x = -0.5095, so the
      stroke is exactly 0.1304. Unlike the carriage this one is NOT bounded by
      a collision - the sweep runs clear through +/-0.2 m - so the limit is the
      geometry of the task, not a hard stop. Two independent things agree on
      it: the base plate's 50 mm bore is centred on that same axis (zero plate
      material within r = 25 mm of it), and the contact count under the bin
      bottoms out at -0.12..-0.13 as its feet straddle that bore.

      q = 0 is parked, q = -0.1304 is under the bit. Sliding under while the
      carriage is low drives the bin into the auger; that pair is deliberately
      NOT disabled in the SRDF.
    -->
    <joint name="drill_container_joint" type="prismatic">
        <parent link="drill_link"/>
        <child link="drill_container_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
        <limit effort="100.0" velocity="0.1" lower="-0.1304" upper="0.0"/>
        <dynamics damping="2.0" friction="1.0"/>
    </joint>
</robot>
